# Every variable here is optional. docker-compose.yml defaults all of them, so a clean clone starts
# with `docker compose up` and nothing else. Copy this file to .env only to override something
# locally; the filled-in copy is never committed. Production does not read a file at all — it takes
# the same names from the GitHub environment, and docker-compose.prod.yml requires them there.
#
# Optional is not unchecked: the application validates the environment it is given as it starts
# (src/config/) and refuses to come up on a value it cannot use, naming the variable. See
# "On reading the environment" in docs/requirements/REQUIREMENTS.md.

# Credentials the compose Postgres service initialises itself with, and — the same three values,
# through a merge key — the ones the application connects with. They are read on the first start
# against an empty volume and ignored afterwards, so changing one later means recreating the
# volume. Defaults: card / card / card. Reserved characters in the password are fine: the
# application percent-encodes each part when it assembles the connection string.
#POSTGRES_USER=
#POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
#POSTGRES_DB=

# Where that database is. The defaults are the compose service name and PostgreSQL's port, so these
# stay unset for every local start. They point the client elsewhere and nothing more, so a database
# outside this project also means dropping the `db` service and the `depends_on` on `app` from the
# compose files — otherwise the local one still starts and is still waited for. Note the host is the
# service name and not localhost — `db` publishes no port, and the Prisma CLI runs through the `app`
# service inside the same network. The host is admitted as a hostname or an IP literal only:
# anything carrying a port, credentials or a path is refused rather than interpolated. Defaults:
# db / 5432.
#POSTGRES_HOST=
#POSTGRES_PORT=

# Port the HTTP server binds to. Default: 3000.
#PORT=

# Apollo reads this; `development` and `production` are the only accepted values. Under
# `production` Apollo serves its own landing page instead of Sandbox and turns introspection off,
# so the app opts back in explicitly — see "On serving Sandbox in production" in
# docs/requirements/REQUIREMENTS.md. docker-compose.yml sets `development` because
# it is the development environment; docker-compose.prod.yml sets `production`. Setting it here
# overrides the default docker-compose.yml substitutes.
#NODE_ENV=
